  "analysis": "The root filesystem shows 100% used (0 available) despite 19GB difference between size and used — likely root-reserved blocks or a quirk of the overlay. The apt cache can't hold 217MB. I need to either free space or find another location. Let me investigate where space is and whether /tmp or another path is usable. Note: a 984G disk with 965G used is unusual for a container; possibly some large file is consuming space that I can clean.",
  "plan": "Check disk for /tmp and /var/cache separately, locate large files/directories that can be cleaned, and possibly set apt to use a different cache dir with more space. First, find what's eating space.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h /tmp /app /var /var/cache/apt 2>&1; echo '---big---'; du -sh /logs/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The root filesystem shows 100% used (0 available) on a 984G fuse-overlayfs, while /logs and /dev/vdb are also full. This appears to be a real space constraint — installing the ~217MB of apt packages (which write to /usr, /bin on the full disk) will likely fail. I need to verify whether I can actually write files, and find a usable location. There are 32G tmpfs mounts available.",
  "plan": "First test if I can write a file to /app (the task output location). Then check the large tmpfs mounts for writability. If the overlay is truly full, I'll need to install binaries into a tmpfs (e.g., /opt on a tmpfs) and run them from there, since the download itself can be staged in tmpfs too. Let me probe the environment.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "touch /app/testfile 2>&1 && echo 'WRITE_OK /app' || echo 'WRITE_FAIL /app'; df -h /sys/fs/cgroup /dev/shm; touch /sys/fs/cgroup/test 2>&1 && echo 'WRITE_OK cgroup' || echo 'WRITE_FAIL cgroup'\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I can write small files to /app, but df reports 0 available on the overlay (984G/966G used). apt refused to install ~217MB of packages. I need to determine the true writeable space before deciding whether to install via apt or fetch binaries directly (yt-dlp as a script, ffmpeg from a PyPI wheel) and keep apt usage minimal. The decisive test is to write a large file.",
  "plan": "Test actual writeable space by creating a ~200MB file in /app. Then df again. This tells me whether I can install tesseract via apt (minimal) or must avoid heavy apt installs. yt-dlp I'll fetch directly as a python script regardless. Clean up the test file after.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "rm -f /app/testfile; dd if=/dev/zero of=/app/bigfile bs=1M count=200 2>&1; echo EXIT=$?; df -h /; rm -f /app/bigfile\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: I CAN write large files to /app (200MB wrote fine, EXIT=0), but df reports 0 available on the fuse-overlayfs root — this is a statvfs reporting quirk. apt's failure was its pre-flight free-space check (statvfs returns 0), not a real write limit. /sys/fs/cgroup is a tmpfs that reports accurate 32G available. I can bypass apt's check by relocating its cache to that tmpfs.",
  "plan": "Create a tmpfs-backed apt cache under /sys/fs/cgroup (which reports real free space), and run apt-get install with -o Dir::Cache pointing there. This makes apt's statvfs disk-space check pass even though it unpacks to the overlay root (which actually can be written). I'll watch whether it proceeds or complains about / space.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /sys/fs/cgroup/aptcache/archives && apt-get -o Dir::Cache=/sys/fs/cgroup/aptcache install -y ffmpeg tesseract-ocr yt-dlp 2>&1 | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 60.0
    }
  ]
}

